{#
    The entries of a `toctree` that prints itself in a document.

    Separate from `menu-item.html.twig` on purpose, and this is the whole of
    the difference. That one writes the rail, which is where a reader goes
    next, and this one writes a list in the prose. The core sends both
    through `menu-level.html.twig`. So a theme that overrides that one file
    has said the same thing about both. That is how a printed list came out
    as a row of filled rail items, every one marked as the current page.

    The link comes from here rather than from the renderer. `renderLink`
    answers `#` for the document in hand — its way to say "you are here". A
    section of that document is that answer plus the anchor. Take the answer
    alone and an entry of the page it is on points at nothing.

    Recursion is the macro that calls itself. A tree nests as deep as it has
    to, and the depth is the author's to choose with `:depth:`.
#}
{% macro entries(items, kids) %}
    {%- import _self as toc -%}
    <ul class="sds-toc__list">
        {%- for item in items %}
            {%- set doc = renderLink(item.url) -%}
            {%- set href = (doc == '#' ? '' : doc) ~ (item.anchor ? '#' ~ item.anchor) -%}
            <li>
                <a class="sds-toc__item" href="{{ href ?: '#' }}">{{ item.value.toString }}</a>
                {%- set children = attribute(item, kids) -%}
                {%- if children %}{{ toc.entries(children, kids) }}{% endif -%}
            </li>
        {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
{% endmacro %}
